WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.709
A pool holds ten loans, and three of them are

00:02.809 --> 00:06.143
going to default. An auditor picks two loans at random.

00:06.243 --> 00:08.703
What's the chance that both are bad ones?

00:10.800 --> 00:14.699
Two laws answer questions like this: one for 'and', one

00:14.799 --> 00:17.753
for 'or'. Plus a trick for 'at least one'.

00:18.850 --> 00:21.447
First pick: three of the ten loans are bad, so

00:21.547 --> 00:24.261
the chance the first one is bad is three tenths.

00:24.583 --> 00:28.570
Picture every possible outcome of the two picks as this square,

00:28.670 --> 00:32.267
with total area one. Its width stands for the first pick.

00:32.366 --> 00:35.125
The left thirty percent of the width is where the first

00:35.225 --> 00:37.308
loan is bad; the rest is where it is good.

00:37.400 --> 00:41.340
Its height stands for the second pick, and inside each strip the

00:41.440 --> 00:45.065
pool is different. If the first loan was bad, two bad loans

00:45.165 --> 00:48.854
remain among nine, so only the bottom two ninths of the left

00:48.954 --> 00:52.389
strip has a bad second loan as well. On the right, after

00:52.489 --> 00:55.167
a good first loan, it would be three ninths.

00:55.266 --> 00:58.796
Both loans are bad only in the red corner: three tenths

00:58.896 --> 01:02.623
wide and two ninths tall. Its area, three tenths times two

01:02.723 --> 01:06.384
ninths, one in fifteen, is the chance of both. That's the

01:06.484 --> 01:10.740
multiplicative law: a fraction of a fraction. The second factor is

01:10.840 --> 01:15.161
conditional, because the first pick changed the pool. If the events

01:15.261 --> 01:19.120
were independent, it would just be the ordinary probability.

01:20.216 --> 01:23.621
Now 'or'. Sixty percent of a bank's customers have

01:23.721 --> 01:27.476
its credit card, thirty percent have its mortgage. What

01:27.576 --> 01:29.860
share has at least one of the two?

01:30.950 --> 01:34.199
Adding gives ninety percent. But look at the overlap.

01:34.299 --> 01:37.549
Customers with both products were counted once in the

01:37.649 --> 01:39.888
first circle and again in the second.

01:41.483 --> 01:44.877
So subtract the overlap once: sixty plus thirty minus

01:44.977 --> 01:49.163
twenty, seventy percent. That's the additive law. The subtraction

01:49.263 --> 01:52.328
isn't a fudge; it just repairs the double count.

01:53.916 --> 01:57.552
Finally, 'at least one'. A payment system runs on ten independent

01:57.652 --> 02:00.942
servers, and each one fails on a given day with probability

02:01.042 --> 02:03.873
five percent. What's the chance at least one fails?

02:03.966 --> 02:08.544
'At least one' is a mess: exactly one, or exactly two, or exactly

02:08.644 --> 02:13.439
three, and so on. Its opposite is clean: none fail. And none failing

02:13.539 --> 02:17.685
is an 'and' of ten independent events, so we just multiply.

02:17.783 --> 02:21.971
After ten servers, the chance nothing fails is point nine

02:22.071 --> 02:25.958
five to the tenth, about sixty percent. So the chance

02:26.058 --> 02:29.569
that at least one fails is one minus that: about

02:29.669 --> 02:33.330
forty percent. Small risks, repeated, add up fast.

02:34.416 --> 02:37.847
So: for 'and', multiply, zooming in as you go.

02:37.947 --> 02:41.763
For 'or', add, but subtract what you counted twice.

02:41.863 --> 02:45.601
And for 'at least one', go through the complement.
